00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:08.720
SPEAKER: Google
Kubernetes Engine,

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:11.420
or GKE's, node
auto-upgrades feature

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:13.460
helps to keep your
cluster nodes up

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:16.100
to date with the cluster
control plane version

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:19.910
whenever the control
plane is updated.

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:23.630
Node auto-upgrades
provide several benefits--

00:00:23.630 --> 00:00:25.850
lower management overhead.

00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:29.150
With this, you don't need to
manually update the nodes when

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:31.550
the control plane is upgraded.

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:34.580
Better security--
with this feature, GKE

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:37.610
ensures that the security
updates are automatically

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:39.800
applied.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:42.320
Ease of use--
provide a simple way

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:45.830
to keep your nodes up to date
with the latest Kubernetes

00:00:45.830 --> 00:00:47.780
features.

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:51.410
In this video, we will cover how
to configure node auto-upgrades

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:52.190
in GKE.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:57.070
If you are running
a GKE cluster,

00:00:57.070 --> 00:00:59.140
the two most common
ways to configure it

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:05.140
are either by using Google Cloud
console or Google Cloud CLI.

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:08.950
First, we will learn how to
enable node auto-upgrades

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:13.830
for an existing node for
use in Google Cloud Console.

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:17.940
In the Google Cloud Console,
go to Kubernetes Engine page.

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:23.900
In the cluster list, select the
cluster you want to configure.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:26.300
Click the Nodes tab.

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:28.700
Then, click the node
pool for which you

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:32.710
want to enable auto-upgrade.

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:35.350
On the Node Pool Details
page, click Edit.

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:40.260
Under the Management
section, select

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:44.420
the Enable
Audo-Upgrade checkbox.

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:46.230
Click Save.

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:48.990
Wait for confirmation
notification.

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:50.040
There you go.

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:55.870
You have successfully enabled
node pools to be auto-upgraded.

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:59.230
Now, let's learn how to
enable node auto-upgrades

00:01:59.230 --> 00:02:00.490
using Google Cloud CLI.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:06.020
We will use Cloud
Shell in this example.

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:08.509
Before running the
Google Cloud CLI,

00:02:08.509 --> 00:02:11.450
ensures that you have the
following information--

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:15.770
notebook, name, your
cluster name, and the zone

00:02:15.770 --> 00:02:19.310
where the node pool is located.

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:20.465
Run the following command.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:30.180
Finally, you can verify
that your node pools have

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:33.660
auto-upgrade enabled by using
G-cloud DESCRIBE command

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:34.290
as follows.

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:39.930
Congratulations.

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:44.340
You have successfully enabled
node pools to be auto-upgraded.

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:47.520
One last note-- clusters
enrolled to our release

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:49.440
channels can also be upgraded.

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:53.010
In this scenario, auto-upgrade
is enabled by default

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:54.840
and cannot be disabled.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:59.310
Finally, if you are running
a GKE Autopilot cluster,

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:02.910
node auto-upgrades are enabled
by default and cannot be

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:03.900
overridden.

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:07.695
Check out the documentation for
more information on GKE node

00:03:07.695 --> 00:03:08.880
auto-upgrades.

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:12.530
[MUSIC PLAYING]

